   .table-expanded-content with `position: sticky; left: 12px` and
   `width: var(--table-visible-width, calc(100vw - 360px))`.

   Inside the `overflow: hidden` table cell (colspan=11 row) that sticky
   offset shifts the whole panel 12px to the right: the cell's natural
   14px + 14px padding (and the `wrapper.clientWidth - 28` width budget in
   commercial.php) become 26px left inset vs 3px right inset \2014  the right
   side reads flush/clipped while the left keeps a generous gap. The sticky
   pin never engages usefully here (the cell itself scrolls away with the
   table), so `left: 0` removes the shift with no downside.

   The old `calc(100vw - 360px)` fallback also overshot the real shell
   chrome (~408px of rail + sub-sidebar + paddings), letting the panel's
   right padding get cut off by the card's overflow-hidden whenever
   --table-visible-width had not been synced yet. The narrower fallback
   keeps the panel inside the card in every state.

   `html .table-expanded-content` (0,1,1) beats the later-loaded
   commercial.model.css rule (0,1,0) without !important, and the custom
   property is still honored, so the JS width sync keeps working. */
html .table-expanded-content {
  left: 0;
  width: var(--table-visible-width, calc(100vw - 440px));
  max-width: var(--table-visible-width, calc(100vw - 440px));
}
@media (max-width: 1023px) {
  html .table-expanded-content {
    width: var(--table-visible-width, calc(100vw - 140px));
    max-width: var(--table-visible-width, calc(100vw - 140px));
  }
}

